\ this ( -- v ) / that ( -- v ): the demonstratives. The pair fixes the top two
\ stack values at the first mention of either word in the clause and holds them
\ for the rest of it; this pushes the first value, that the second. Both are
\ non-consuming and repeatable. A clause is one input line at top level and one
\ activation in compiled code, where the mention that fixes the pair must sit at
\ the top level of the body.

\ === the pair fixes at the first mention on the line ===
5 this * . cr          \ 25
clear
1 2 this .s cr         \ 1 2 2
clear
1 2 that .s cr         \ 1 2 1
clear
1 2 this that - . cr   \ 1
clear

\ === the values hold for the rest of the line ===
1 2 this 3 4 this .s cr  \ 1 2 2 3 4 2 (the second mention reads the fixed value)
clear
9 100 this + . cr      \ 200 (this names the 100, the top where it was mentioned)
clear
3 4 that this + + . cr   \ 11 (one fixing serves both words: 3 + 4, then + 4)
clear

\ === heap values alias, like dup ===
[ 1 2 3 ] this size . cr   \ 3
clear
[ 1 2 3 ] this 0 @i . cr   \ 1
clear

\ === a fixed value outlives the stack cell it came from ===
[ 4 5 ] this drop drop gc this size . cr   \ 2
clear

\ === a failed line restores the stack; the next line fixes afresh ===
8
1 0 %                  \ (division by zero; the line fails and the stack is restored)
this . cr              \ 8
clear

\ === in a file, each line fixes its own ===
": double this + ;
7 double . cr
5 this this * . cr
1 2 this that - . cr
" "/tmp/water_139_demonstratives.h2o" write-file
"/tmp/water_139_demonstratives.h2o" load    \ 14, then 25, then 1
clear

\ === definitions: the pair fixes where it is mentioned in the body ===
: doubled 2 * this + ;
7 doubled . cr         \ 28 (this names the doubled value, not the entry value)
: squared this * ;
5 squared . cr         \ 25
: pair-diff this that - ;
3 4 pair-diff . cr     \ 1
clear

\ === the fixed value survives the stack moving on ===
: kept this drop drop this 100 + ;
7 kept . cr            \ 107 (the second mention reads the slot, the stack being empty)
clear

\ === one frame carries user locals and the pair ===
: framed | >x | x 2 * this + ;
6 framed . cr          \ 24
' framed see-compiled
clear

\ === quotations read the definition's fixed values ===
: mapped 2 * this drop [ 1 2 3 ] [: drop this :] map ;
7 mapped . cr          \ [ 14 14 14 ]
clear

\ === mentions after the fixing one may sit inside branches ===
: branchy this 5 > if this 2 * else this 10 + then ;
9 branchy . cr         \ 18
3 branchy . cr         \ 13
clear

\ === early exit unwinds the frame ===
: early this 5 > if "big" exit then this 2 * ;
9 early . cr           \ big
3 early . cr           \ 6
clear

\ === recursion fixes per activation ===
: countdown this 1 > if this 1 - countdown then this ;
3 countdown .s cr      \ 3 2 1 1 2 3 (the trailing mentions read 1, 2, 3 — one fixing per activation)
clear

\ === inline splice fixes at the call site ===
: sq this this * ; inline
: user 1 + sq ;
4 user . cr            \ 25
clear

\ === see shows the typed source; see-compiled shows the frame ===
' squared see
' squared see-compiled

\ === a local named this shadows the demonstrative ===
: shadowed | >this | this 100 + ;
1 shadowed . cr        \ 101
clear

\ === a top-level quotation fixes on the line that runs it ===
5 [: this 2 * :] execute . cr  \ 10
clear

\ === errors: too few values, or a fixing mention that reaches nothing ===
this                   \ (nothing on the stack where this is mentioned)
that                   \ (nothing on the stack; that needs 2)
7
that                   \ (one value; that needs 2)
clear
: fix-in-branch dup 1 > if this 2 * nip then ;   \ (not the top level of the body)
: fix-in-quotation [: this :] execute ;          \ (same)
